    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm running an organic kale operation up in the PNW and I'm running into some nitrogen issues. My soil pH is sitting around 6.2 right now and I've been hitting it with compost, but the leaves are still looking pretty pale. I'm looking to supplement the nitrogen directly so I don't mess with the pH too much. Between bone meal and blood meal, which one would you recommend for a quick nitrogen boost without spiking the pH?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A nitrogen boost in the PNW, eh? I'd recommend bone meal over blood meal for a quick nitrogen fix. Bone meal is a slow-release fertilizer, which means it'll provide a steady supply of phosphorus and potassium, as well as nitrogen, over a longer period. It's also less likely to spike your soil pH, as it's more alkaline in nature. Just be sure to mix it in well, as the bone fragments can be a bit coarse.",
